var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-selene"]=(()=>{var a=Object.defineProperty;var r=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(o,e)=>{for(var n in e)a(o,n,{get:e[n],enumerable:!0})},m=(o,e,n,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let t of h(e))!d.call(o,t)&&t!==n&&a(o,t,{get:()=>e[t],enumerable:!(i=r(e,t))||i.enumerable});return o};var c=o=>m(a({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),o);var u={};l(u,{journey:()=>s});var s={id:"deity-selene",kind:"deity",paths:["hellenic","wicca"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/selene",title:"Selene: The Moon Herself",summary:"A lunar month with Selene, the Greek goddess who is the moon: her hymns and the story of Endymion, the honest truth about her slim ancient cult, how she was later blended with Artemis and Hekate, and a practice of watching the moon each night, from the first crescent to the full.",welcome:`This is a month with Selene, the goddess who is the moon itself: not a goddess who rules the moon, but the shining thing in the sky, driving her horses across the night. The Greeks greeted her when she rose, measured their months by her, and sang of her beauty, but they rarely built her temples.

That makes her an unusual goddess to dedicate to. Most of what we know of her is poetry. Most of how people honour her now is modern moon devotion, which is real and lovely, but new. This journey will be clear about which is which.

It is a self-dedication, not an initiation. It is best begun on the Noumenia, the evening the first thin crescent appears after the new moon, so that the month carries you to the full moon for your dedication and back to the dark again. You need a window or a patch of sky, a bowl of water, a lamp and a notebook. Clouds are allowed. She is there behind them.`,about:"Selene is the Greek moon goddess, daughter of the Titans Hyperion and Theia, sister of Helios the sun and Eos the dawn. Hesiod names her in his Theogony around 700 BCE; the Homeric Hymn to Selene describes her bathing in Ocean and driving her long-maned horses up the sky at full moon, \u201Ca sure token and a sign to mortal men.\u201D Her most famous story is her love for the shepherd Endymion, who sleeps forever in a cave on Mount Latmos while she visits him. In actual cult she had very little: a few shrines, statues in the marketplace of Elis, offerings at the new and full moon, and her place in the calendar, since every Greek month began with the first sight of her crescent. In later centuries she was merged with Artemis, Hekate and Persephone, especially in the magical papyri of Roman Egypt, and the Romans honoured her as Luna. Today Hellenic polytheists honour her at the Noumenia and the full moon, and many witches, Wiccans and moon-watchers keep monthly devotions to the moon that are modern, sincere and widely shared. This journey keeps Selene as herself, the moon, rather than as a stand-in for every lunar goddess.",notes:["Selene had a slim ancient cult. Most devotional practice to her today is modern, built on poetry, the calendar and a few cult traces. The journey says so at each step.","Selene was blended with Artemis and Hekate mainly in later antiquity. Earlier Greeks kept them distinct. The modern \u201Ctriple moon goddess\u201D is a twentieth-century idea.","The Wiccan rite called Drawing Down the Moon is initiatory and oathbound in traditional covens; this journey does not reproduce it. The phrase itself comes from ancient Greek jokes about Thessalian witches.","If you are outside at night to watch the moon, stay in safe, lit places, tell someone where you are, and dress warmly.","White lilies and some night-blooming flowers are toxic to cats and dogs. Choose pet-safe white flowers such as roses or true jasmine (Jasminum) if you share your home with animals; \u201CCarolina jasmine\u201D and \u201Cstar jasmine\u201D are different plants, and Carolina jasmine is poisonous."],holyDays:["noumenia","mounichia","deipnon"],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"The first crescent",intro:"Greeks began each month when someone saw the first thin crescent of the moon after sunset. So the journey begins on that evening, the Noumenia, with a greeting. You are only saying hello to her.",days:2,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"selene-threshold-find",title:"Find the young moon",instructions:"Check a moon calendar or the app for the next new moon. On the first or second evening after it, go outside or to a west-facing window about half an hour after sunset and look low in the western sky for a very thin crescent. It sets soon after the sun, so be quick. If you cannot see it, look for the moon the next night instead.",times:1,prompt:"Did I see her? What did it feel like to look for something so faint?"}],rite:{title:"Greeting the new moon",summary:"A short Greek-style greeting on the Noumenia: purify, begin with Hestia, raise your hands to the first crescent, greet Selene by her titles and offer a little water and honey.",minutes:15,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of fresh water for khernips",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A match, or a sprig of bay or rosemary to quench in it",ref:"traditional/herb/bay"},{name:"A small lamp, tealight or electric candle"},{name:"A small cup of water with a little honey stirred in"},{name:"A small dish",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"A white flower, pet-safe if you have animals",optional:!0}],preparation:"Choose a window or a spot outside where you can see the western sky after sunset. Wash your face and hands in ordinary water. Set the lamp, cup and dish nearby.",steps:[{title:"Make khernips",instructions:"Light the match or the tip of the bay sprig and plunge it into the bowl of water. Wash your hands in it and touch a few drops to your face.",spoken:"With clean hands I greet the new month."},{title:"Begin with Hestia",instructions:"Light the lamp. Pour a drop into the dish for Hestia.",spoken:"Hestia, first and last, keeper of this house, I begin with you.",light:!0},{title:"Greet Selene",instructions:"Turn toward the crescent, or the place in the sky where she is. Raise your hands with palms up, toward her. Plato mentions people bowing to the sun and moon as they rose and set, and in the ancient Mediterranean people sometimes kissed their hand toward a god in worship; you may greet her either way.",spoken:"Selene, bright moon, daughter of Hyperion, sister of the sun: hail to you. I am [your name]. A new month begins with your light. I would like to know you.",minutes:2},{title:"Offer to her",instructions:"Pour most of the honey-water into the dish. Lay the white flower beside it if you have one. Then simply watch her for a few minutes, until she sets or the clouds cover her.",minutes:4,timed:!0},{title:"Close with Hestia",instructions:"Pour the last drop for Hestia. Thank them both. Put out the lamp.",spoken:"Hestia, last as you were first. Selene, thank you for your light.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Pour the dish outside onto earth or into a plant, under the night sky if you can. Pour the khernips away. Write the date and the moon\u2019s phase at the top of a new page in your journal: this is the first night of your moon record.",safety:"Never leave a flame unattended, and keep it away from curtains at an open window. If you go outside after dark, stay in safe places and tell someone. Keep lilies away from cats."}},{kind:"study",title:"What the poets said",intro:"About ten days of reading while the moon grows, one lesson every two or three days. First who Selene is, then her myths, then her actual cult and its limits. Keep looking up each night as you read.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"selene-who",title:"The moon as a goddess",summary:"Her family, her hymn, her chariot and emblems, and what makes her different from other lunar goddesses.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"The sister of the sun",body:`Selene\u2019s name simply means \u201Cmoon,\u201D from the Greek selas, \u201Cbrightness.\u201D Hesiod\u2019s Theogony, from around 700 BCE, says that the Titaness Theia bore to Hyperion \u201Cgreat Helius (Sun) and clear Selene (Moon) and Eos (Dawn) who shines upon all.\u201D So she is a Titan\u2019s daughter, older than the Olympians, and one of three siblings who carry light across the sky: the sun by day, the moon by night, and the dawn between.

This matters. Selene is not a goddess with the moon as one of her interests, the way later writers saw Artemis or Hekate. She is the moon. When a Greek looked up at night, they saw Selene herself. Another name for her was Mene, a plainer word for moon and for month.`},{heading:"The hymn",body:`The Homeric Hymn to Selene is short and beautiful. It tells how \u201Cfrom her immortal head a radiance is shown from heaven and embraces earth.\u201D When she has bathed in the waters of Ocean, she puts on shining robes and yokes her horses and drives them \u201Cat eventime in the mid-month,\u201D when \u201Cher great orbit is full and then her beams shine brightest as she increases. So she is a sure token and a sign to mortal men.\u201D

The hymn adds that Zeus once loved her, and that she bore him a daughter, Pandia, \u201Cexceeding lovely amongst the deathless gods.\u201D It ends: \u201CHail, white-armed goddess, bright Selene, mild, bright-tressed queen!\u201D Notice \u201Ca sure token and a sign.\u201D For the Greeks, the moon\u2019s main gift was regularity: she marked the months, the festivals, the time to sow and to pay debts.`},{heading:"Chariot, crescent and horses",body:`In art Selene is a young woman with a crescent on her brow or behind her shoulders, a billowing veil arched over her head like the night sky, sometimes holding a torch. She drives a chariot of two horses, or rides a single horse or a mule, or occasionally a bull, whose horns echo the crescent. On the east pediment of the Parthenon, the sun\u2019s horses rose at one corner and Selene\u2019s horses sank at the other, framing the birth of Athena between dawn and moonset.

No plants or stones are clearly linked to her in ancient sources. Modern devotees use white flowers that open at night, jasmine, white roses, moonstone, selenite (whose name comes from hers, via the Greek for \u201Cmoonstone\u201D) and silver. These are good modern choices, but they are modern.`},{heading:"Selene, Artemis and Hekate",body:`You will often read that Selene, Artemis and Hekate are the three faces of one moon goddess. In early Greek religion they were distinct. Artemis was a goddess of the wild, the hunt and young girls; Hekate a goddess of thresholds and crossroads; Selene the moon. Over time, especially from the fourth century BCE onward, writers began to identify Artemis with the moon, as Apollo was identified with the sun. In the Greek Magical Papyri of Roman Egypt, Selene, Hekate, Artemis and Persephone flow together into one great lunar goddess.

The \u201Ctriple moon goddess\u201D of maiden, mother and crone, waxing, full and waning, is a much later idea, popularised in the twentieth century by Robert Graves and the goddess movement. It is a modern system, not an ancient one. Honouring Selene as herself is honest, and it also means you meet a goddess who is gentler and stranger than the grand composite: simply the moon, rising.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Hesiod\u2019s Theogony and the Homeric Hymn to Selene give her family and image. The Parthenon pediments and many vases and reliefs show her chariot and crescent. Her merging with Artemis, Hekate and Persephone is documented in later texts and the magical papyri.",unknown:"The date of the Homeric Hymn to Selene is uncertain, possibly as late as the Hellenistic period. Pandia is otherwise little known, and may be a personified festival. Modern plant and crystal lists are modern."},inPractice:"Hellenic polytheists usually honour Selene as herself, and Artemis and Hekate separately. Begin a simple moon record this week: each night, note the date, the phase, where in the sky you saw her and how she looked. This is the oldest kind of attention to her.",reflect:["What changes when you think of the moon as a goddess herself, rather than a goddess\u2019s symbol?","Did you arrive with the triple goddess in mind? How do you feel meeting Selene alone?"],further:["Homeric Hymn 32, To Selene, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), free on Project Gutenberg","Hesiod, Theogony 371\u2013374","Robert Graves, The White Goddess (1948), as the source of the modern triple moon"],library:["traditional/deity/selene","traditional/deity/artemis","traditional/deity/hekate","traditional/crystal/moonstone","traditional/crystal/selenite"]},{id:"selene-myths",title:"Endymion, Pan and the witches of Thessaly",summary:"Her love stories, the old joke about drawing down the moon, and what they tell us about her.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The sleeping shepherd",body:`Selene\u2019s great love was Endymion, a beautiful young man, in some versions a king of Elis and in others a shepherd or hunter on Mount Latmos in Caria, in what is now south-west Turkey. She loved him so much that she asked Zeus to grant him eternal sleep, ageless and undying, so that she could visit him every night in his cave. Other versions say Zeus offered him a choice, and he chose to sleep forever.

The poet Apollonius of Rhodes, in the third century BCE, has Selene herself remember it, watching Medea run through the night to meet Jason: \u201Cnor do I alone burn with love for fair Endymion.\u201D Pausanias heard in Elis that she bore him fifty daughters, often taken to be the fifty lunar months of the Olympic cycle. At Herakleia under Latmos there was a shrine where Endymion\u2019s tomb was shown.`},{heading:"What Endymion means",body:`People have read the story in many ways. Some see the moon and sleep, two powers of the night joined. Some see the moon visiting the dark side of a mountain each month. Some see a story about love that cannot fully meet: she shines, he sleeps, and it is only ever night. Keats wrote a long poem about it in 1818, and it became a favourite of Romantic painters.

For a devotee, Endymion is a reminder that Selene is a goddess of rest, sleep and dreams, and of a love that keeps coming back faithfully, phase after phase. Many people honour her at bedtime for exactly this reason.`},{heading:"Pan and the fleece",body:"A stranger story, told by the Roman poet Virgil (the later scholar Macrobius says he took it from the Greek poet Nicander), says that Pan, the goat-legged god of the wild, won Selene by offering her a beautiful white fleece, or by disguising himself in one. The moon, who loves white and shining things, came down to him in the woods. It is a small, odd tale, but it explains why white wool and white things are sometimes given to her by modern devotees."},{heading:"Drawing down the moon",body:`The women of Thessaly in northern Greece had a reputation for witchcraft, and above all for being able to draw down the moon from the sky. Aristophanes, in his comedy the Clouds (423 BCE), has a debt-ridden old man plan to hire a Thessalian witch to pull down the moon and lock it in a box, so that the month will never end and his interest will never fall due. Plato mentions the same belief more seriously, saying that the Thessalian women who draw down the moon pay for it with something dear to them.

Eclipses were sometimes blamed on such witches, and Roman writers describe crowds clashing bronze to help the moon in her trouble. This is where the modern Wiccan phrase \u201Cdrawing down the moon\u201D comes from, though the Wiccan rite is a twentieth-century creation with a different meaning, and in traditional covens it is oathbound. In Greek eyes, pulling the moon down was dangerous meddling with a goddess who should be left to drive her horses in peace.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Endymion appears in Sappho (according to ancient commentators), Apollonius (Argonautica 4.57\u201358), Pausanias (5.1.4\u20135) and many others. Pan and the fleece is in Virgil, Georgics 3.391\u2013393. Aristophanes\u2019 Clouds (749\u2013755) and Plato\u2019s Gorgias (513a) mention the Thessalian witches.",unknown:"Whether Endymion had a real cult beyond his tomb shrines is unclear. The fifty daughters as lunar months is a modern interpretation. Macrobius (Saturnalia 5.22.9\u201310) names Nicander as Virgil\u2019s source for the Pan story, but Nicander\u2019s version is lost."},inPractice:"Some devotees honour Selene with a quiet bedtime greeting, asking for good sleep and gentle dreams, and keep a dream journal beside the bed as a lunar practice. Try it this week: before sleep, look out at the night, greet her, and write down what you dream. If you make the dream pillow, leave out the mugwort if you are pregnant, trying to conceive or breastfeeding, or allergic to ragweed and daisy-family plants; lavender or chamomile alone works well, and keep dried herbs away from cats.",reflect:["What would it mean to love something that only meets you at night?","How do you feel about the Greek idea that pulling the moon down is meddling, not power?"],further:["Apollonius of Rhodes, Argonautica 4.54\u201365, trans. R. C. Seaton (1912), free on Project Gutenberg","Pausanias, Description of Greece 5.1.4\u20135","Aristophanes, Clouds 749\u2013755","John Keats, Endymion (1818)"],library:["traditional/deity/selene","traditional/herb/jasmine","traditional/herb/mugwort"],premade:["hedge-dream-pillow"]},{id:"selene-cult",title:"A slim cult: how Selene was honoured",summary:"The few traces of real worship, the lunar calendar, the Noumenia and the full moon, and Roman Luna.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Honest about the evidence",body:`Here is the plain truth. Selene had very little organised worship in ancient Greece. There was no great temple of Selene in Athens, no city festival named after her, and very few dedications. Some scholars think this is because she was too obviously a natural object: you did not need a temple to see the moon. Others note that the Greeks honoured the sun and moon more privately, with greetings and gestures rather than sacrifices.

Plato, in the Laws, mentions people bowing and praying to the sun and moon at their rising and setting, Greeks and foreigners alike, as an ordinary thing. That may be the best picture we have of everyday devotion to Selene: a gesture, a word, a kiss blown from the hand, as she came up over the hills.`},{heading:"The few traces",body:`There are some traces. Pausanias saw stone images of the sun and the moon in the marketplace of Elis, with rays on his head and horns on hers. Near Thalamai in Laconia there was a dream oracle, where people slept and the goddess answered them in dreams. Pausanias calls it a sanctuary of Ino, but bronze statues of Pasiphae, \u201Cthe all-shining,\u201D and Helios stood there, and he says Pasiphae is a title of the moon; Plutarch calls it the oracle of Pasiphae. In Attica and elsewhere there are a few inscriptions and altars to Selene, and Orphic poetry gave her a hymn in which she was offered aromatic incense.

The Orphic Hymn to the Moon, in Thomas Taylor\u2019s 1792 translation, begins \u201CHear, Goddess queen, diffusing silver light\u201D and calls her \u201Cfair lamp of Night.\u201D The Orphic hymns are late, probably from the Roman period, and were used by a particular group, but they show that someone was praying to her.`},{heading:"The month itself",body:`Selene\u2019s biggest place in Greek religion was not a temple but the calendar. Every Greek month was lunar. The Noumenia, the first day, began when the young crescent was seen; it was a day for household gods and a good day to start things. The middle of the month, the dichomenia, was full moon, and many festivals fell on or near it. The last day, the dark of the moon, was Hekate\u2019s Deipnon, when the house was cleansed.

In Athens, the Mounichia festival of Artemis, at the full moon of spring, featured round cakes ringed with little torches called amphiphontes, \u201Cshining all round,\u201D said to look like the full moon. That was a festival of Artemis, not Selene, but it shows how naturally the Greeks honoured the full moon with light and round cakes. Lexicographers also mention cakes called selenai, shaped like the moon.`},{heading:"Roman Luna",body:`The Romans had a moon goddess of their own, Luna, with a temple on the Aventine hill whose anniversary fell on 31 March, and a temple on the Palatine to Luna Noctiluca, \u201Cthe night-shining,\u201D which the scholar Varro says was lit up at night. Later Romans identified Diana with the moon as Greeks did with Artemis. The Roman day of the moon, dies Lunae, survives as Monday in English, lundi in French and luned\xEC in Italian.

So if you honour Selene on Mondays, you are following a Roman-era naming of the week rather than a Greek custom, since the Greeks did not use a seven-day week. That is a perfectly good modern practice. Just know where it comes from.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Pausanias 6.24.6 (Elis) and 3.26.1 (Thalamai) describe statues and an oracle. Plato, Laws 887e, mentions prayers at moonrise. The lunar month, Noumenia and Deipnon are well attested. The Mounichia cakes are described by later writers. Luna\u2019s temples are recorded in Roman calendars.",unknown:"We do not know whether individuals made regular offerings to Selene, or what they said. The Orphic hymns are late and specialised. Most claims about \u201Cancient full moon rites to Selene\u201D are modern reconstructions or inventions."},inPractice:"Hellenic polytheists most often honour Selene at the Noumenia, with a greeting and a little honey or incense, and at the full moon. Some bake round honey cakes for the full moon, a modern custom inspired by the Mounichia. Mark the next full moon in your calendar: that is your dedication night.",reflect:["How does it feel to honour a goddess whose cult was mostly a gesture?","What would change if you began each month with a greeting to her?"],further:["Plato, Laws 887e","Pausanias, Description of Greece 3.26.1 and 6.24.6","Orphic Hymn 9, To the Moon, trans. Thomas Taylor (1792)","Jon D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Civil Calendar of the Athenian Year (1975)"],library:["traditional/deity/selene","traditional/deity/artemis","traditional/deity/diana","traditional/herb/frankincense"],premade:["folk-noumenia-blessing"]},{id:"selene-today",title:"Moon devotion today, and her shrine",summary:"Hellenic, Wiccan and eclectic moon practice, where they differ, what is modern, and how to set up her shrine.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Three kinds of moon devotion",body:`Moon devotion is one of the most popular spiritual practices today, and it comes from several places. Hellenic polytheists honour Selene at the Noumenia and full moon, as one goddess among many, and keep her separate from Artemis and Hekate. Their practice is modest: a greeting, honey, incense, a hymn.

Wiccans and many witches keep esbats, rites at the full moon and sometimes the new moon. In Wicca the moon is usually a symbol of the Goddess, who is honoured under many names, of which Selene can be one. This practice took shape in the 1940s and 1950s with Gerald Gardner and Doreen Valiente. Eclectic moon-watchers and astrologers add moon water, intention-setting at the new moon and release at the full, and phase-based correspondences. All of these are modern and widely shared. None of them is the ancient Greek cult of Selene, but many draw on it sincerely.`},{heading:"Where they differ",body:`The biggest difference is about who the moon is. For a Hellenic polytheist, the moon is Selene, a particular goddess with her own family and story. For many Wiccans, the moon is a face of the one Goddess. For many eclectic practitioners, the moon is a source of energy that powers spells and moods. These are different ideas, and they lead to different practices.

You can borrow from all of them, as long as you are honest about it. If you make moon water, you are following a modern practice, not a Greek one. If you set intentions at the new moon, that is modern. If you greet the young crescent on the Noumenia and pour her a little honey, that is about as close to ancient practice as anyone can get.`},{heading:"Setting up her shrine",body:`Selene\u2019s shrine works best by a window, ideally one where the moon can be seen. You need a small lamp or candle holder, a bowl of water, a dish for offerings, and an image of her or of the moon: a crescent, a print of her chariot, or a round mirror to catch the light. Add a white flower, a piece of white wool for the Pan story, and a moonstone or selenite if you like.

White, silver and pale blue are modern colour choices, and the crystals are modern too. Keep your moon record or dream journal beside the shrine. Many devotees keep a small printed lunar calendar there, so the shrine itself marks the month.`},{heading:"Rest as devotion",body:`Selene is a goddess of night, sleep and rhythm. One of the best ways to honour her is simply to keep her rhythm: go to bed at a sensible hour, step outside to look at the sky, notice how the light changes through the month. People who work night shifts, care for babies or live with insomnia sometimes find comfort in her as a companion of the long night.

If sleeplessness or low mood is wearing you down, do talk to a doctor; devotion can sit beside proper care, not replace it. But even for those who sleep well, looking up at the moon each night for a month is a surprisingly powerful practice. Most people in cities have stopped doing it.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Modern Wiccan esbats are documented from the mid-twentieth century. Hellenic polytheist Noumenia and full-moon practices are drawn from the ancient calendar. Moon water and intention-setting are modern practices of the last few decades.",unknown:"There is no evidence that ancient Greeks made moon water, set intentions by phase, or used crystals for Selene. How individual Greeks prayed to her is barely recorded. Modern practices fill those gaps."},inPractice:"Set up her shrine by a window. Decide which practices you want to keep: Greek-style greetings and offerings, Wiccan-style esbats, modern moon water, or a mix, and label them honestly in your own mind. The app\u2019s full moon working is a modern rite that pairs well with this.",reflect:["Which kind of moon devotion feels most like yours?","What would keeping the moon\u2019s rhythm in your own life look like?"],further:["Doreen Valiente, Witchcraft for Tomorrow (1978), on modern esbats","Drew Campbell, Old Stones, New Temples (2000), on Hellenic practice","Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (1999), on the history of modern Paganism"],library:["traditional/deity/selene","traditional/crystal/moonstone","traditional/crystal/selenite","traditional/candle/silver","traditional/candle/white"],premade:["cosmic-full-moon-charging-moon-water","wicca-full-moon-esbat","cosmic-monday-moon-intuition"]}],practices:[{id:"selene-study-record",title:"Keep a moon record",instructions:"Every night of this stage, look for the moon and write a line in your journal: date, phase, where she was, how she looked, what the sky was like. If it is cloudy, write that.",times:10,every:"day",prompt:"Where was she tonight, and how did she look?"},{id:"selene-study-shrine",title:"Set up her shrine by a window",instructions:"After the fourth lesson, set up a small shrine by a window: lamp, bowl of water, dish, image of the moon or of her chariot, a white flower, and your moon record beside it.",times:1,prompt:"What did I put on her shrine, and can I see the sky from it?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Nine nights of the waxing moon",intro:"Nine nights of a short practice as the moon grows toward full, then a lesson on her days and offerings. Time it so the ninth night falls the evening before the full moon, and your dedication on the full moon itself. If clouds hide her, keep the practice anyway.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"selene-days-offerings",title:"Her days, her offerings, and the edges of the moon",summary:"When to honour Selene, what to give her, how to pray to a goddess you can see, and some cautions about the moon in modern practice.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"Her days",body:`The natural holy days of Selene are the moon\u2019s own: the Noumenia, when the first crescent is seen; the full moon, the dichomenia, in the middle of the month; and, more quietly, the dark of the moon, which in Athens belonged to Hekate; many modern devotees think of it as the night Selene rests. The Mounichia, Artemis\u2019s full-moon festival in spring, is a good yearly day to bake round, candle-ringed cakes for the full moon.

Eclipses were frightening in antiquity, a sign of the moon in trouble. Modern devotees often keep a gentle vigil during a lunar eclipse, keeping her company as she darkens and returns. Mondays are a Roman-era choice; Greeks did not use the seven-day week.`},{heading:"What to give",body:`There is little ancient evidence for what was given to Selene specifically, so modern devotees draw on general Greek practice and on her character. Fitting gifts are honey and honey cakes, milk, water, frankincense and other sweet incense (the Orphic hymn asks for aromatics), white flowers, and round cakes for the full moon. Some give white wool or a white ribbon, after the Pan story.

Offerings to Selene are generally treated as those of the gods above: set out, prayed over, and then either left on the shrine until they fade, or, for food, shared with the household afterwards if your practice allows. Many devotees simply leave honey-water out under the moon overnight and pour it onto the earth in the morning.`},{heading:"Praying to a goddess you can see",body:`Selene is one of the few gods you can actually look at while you pray. Use that. Greeks prayed to the sky gods standing, with arms raised and palms up. Face her, raise your hands, and speak to her directly. Plato mentions people bowing to her at rising and setting. A simple bow, or a kiss of the hand toward her, is an old gesture of worship.

Name her with the hymn\u2019s titles: white-armed, bright-tressed, mild, queen. Mention her family, Hyperion, Theia and Helios, as Greek prayer liked to. Then speak plainly. She does not need elaborate words. Ask for good sleep, steady rhythm, clear dreams, and a good month.`},{heading:"Cautions",body:`A few honest cautions. First, the moon does not need to be in any particular phase for you to live your life. Some modern books say you must never start anything on a waning moon, or that the full moon makes everyone wild. These are folklore and modern rules, not facts, and not Greek law either. Use them if they help; drop them if they make you anxious.

Second, leaving water or offerings outside overnight: use a covered jar for moon water if you plan to use it on skin or plants, never drink it unless it is boiled first, and keep honey away from places it will draw wasps or harm pets. Third, keep your feet on the ground. The Greeks thought pulling the moon down was dangerous. Let her be where she is, and look up.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The Noumenia, dichomenia and Deipnon structure is well attested in the Athenian calendar. Arms raised for sky gods, and bowing to the sun and moon, are described in Greek sources. The Orphic Hymn to the Moon specifies aromatics as incense.",unknown:"Specific ancient offerings to Selene are barely recorded. Rules about starting things in a particular moon phase come from later folklore and modern astrology. Whether eclipse vigils were ever devotional, rather than fearful, in Greece is unknown."},inPractice:"In the nine nights below, greet her each night facing the sky with arms raised, give a little honey-water or incense, and add a line to your moon record. On the last night before the full moon, bake or buy a small round cake for the dedication.",reflect:["What is it like to pray while looking at the one you pray to?","Which moon rules from modern books do you follow? Do they help you?"],further:["Jon D. Mikalson, Ancient Greek Religion (2nd ed., 2010), on prayer and gesture","H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (1977), on the Mounichia"],library:["traditional/deity/selene","traditional/herb/frankincense","traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"],premade:["cosmic-lunar-eclipse-vigil","folk-khernips-libation"]}],practices:[{id:"selene-nine-nights",title:"Nine nights of the waxing moon",instructions:"For nine nights, ending the night before the full moon, go to her window or step outside. Wash your hands. Light the lamp and speak Hestia\u2019s name. Find the moon, or the place she should be. Raise your hands, palms up, and greet her with one of her titles. Pour a little honey-water into her dish, or add a pinch of incense. Watch her for three minutes, then write a line in your moon record. Thank her, put out the lamp, and pour the dish onto earth outside in the morning.",times:9,every:"day",prompt:"How has she grown since last night? What has grown in me?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"The bright night",intro:`At the full moon everything is lit, and things that were hidden become clear. Before you make a promise, take a night or two to look at the month honestly in that light. A dedication is a promise to keep showing up, so it is only worth making if you mean it.

Stopping here is a good outcome. So is waiting another month, or realising that Artemis, Hekate, another god, another path or no dedication at all suits you better. The moon goes and comes back every month. There is always another full moon.`,days:2,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"selene-discern-reread",title:"Read your moon record",instructions:"Read your moon record from the first crescent to now. Notice which nights you kept and which you skipped, what you felt and what you saw.",times:1,prompt:"Reading back over the month, what do I see about myself and her?"}],questions:["Did I keep the nightly practice because I wanted to, or because I felt I should?","Am I drawn to Selene herself, or to the moon in general, or to a Wiccan or eclectic Goddess? Which relationship do I actually want?","Am I at peace with how little we know of her ancient cult, and that most of my practice will be modern?","Can I keep a monthly greeting at the Noumenia and the full moon for a year, in my life as it is now?","Has looking at the moon each night helped my rest and rhythm, or become another pressure?","Would I be relieved or disappointed if I chose to wait? What does that tell me?"]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The dedication at the full moon",intro:`If you have chosen to go on, make your dedication on the night of the full moon, when, as the hymn says, \u201Cher great orbit is full and then her beams shine brightest.\u201D This is a self-dedication, a promise you make on your own behalf. It is not an initiation, and it does not make you a priestess, a witch of any lineage, or a member of any coven or order. Covens that initiate have their own rites, including their own moon rites, and this does not replace them.

Ancient Greeks rarely dedicated themselves to a single god, and almost nobody dedicated to Selene. You are doing something new, in an old spirit.`,days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-dedication to Selene",summary:"Purify, begin with Hestia, greet the full moon with arms raised, call Selene by the hymn\u2019s titles, make your promise, offer a round cake and honey-water, and sit in her light.",minutes:40,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of fresh water and a match or bay sprig for khernips",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A small lamp, tealight or electric candle"},{name:"A small round honey cake or biscuit, with a tiny candle or tealight beside it"},{name:"A cup of water with honey, or milk with honey"},{name:"A dish for offerings",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"A white flower, pet-safe if you have animals"},{name:"Your moon record and written promise"},{name:"A little frankincense on a heatproof burner",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense",optional:!0},{name:"A moonstone or piece of selenite",ref:"traditional/crystal/moonstone",optional:!0}],preparation:"Write your promise in your own words, simple and doable: for example, to greet her at each Noumenia and full moon, to keep a moon record, to honour rest and rhythm, and to learn her honestly. Set up by her window, or outside where you can see the full moon rising. Moonrise at full moon is close to sunset. Wash and dress in clean clothes; white or pale clothing suits her.",steps:[{title:"Purify",instructions:"Make khernips by quenching the flame in the water. Wash your hands and touch your face.",spoken:"With clean hands I come to the full moon."},{title:"Begin with Hestia",instructions:"Light the lamp. Pour a drop for Hestia.",spoken:"Hestia, first and last, I begin with you.",light:!0},{title:"Greet her rising",instructions:"Face the moon. Raise your arms, palms up. Bow, as Plato says people did at moonrise, or kiss your hand toward her. Add incense if you are using it.",spoken:"Hail, white-armed goddess, bright Selene, mild, bright-tressed queen! Daughter of Hyperion and Theia, sister of the sun and the dawn, driver of the long-maned horses: hear me.",minutes:2},{title:"Tell her the month",instructions:"Hold your moon record. Tell her briefly what this month with her has been: the first crescent, the nights you watched, what changed.",minutes:3},{title:"Make your promise",instructions:"Read your promise aloud to her.",spoken:"This is my promise, made freely under your full light. Be its sure token and its sign."},{title:"Offer the round cake",instructions:"Light the tiny candle beside the round cake, an echo of the Mounichia cakes that shone all round. Place the cake, the white flower and most of the honey-water on her dish."},{title:"Sit in her light",instructions:"Sit quietly in the moonlight, or facing where she is behind the clouds. Hold the moonstone if you have one. Let your eyes rest on her.",minutes:8,timed:!0},{title:"Close with Hestia",instructions:"Pour the last drop for Hestia. Thank both goddesses. Put out the small candle and the lamp.",spoken:"Hestia, last as you were first. Selene, it is done.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Leave the cake and flower on her shrine overnight. In the morning, pour the honey-water onto earth and put the cake into the compost. Some devotees instead share a little of the cake in the morning, which suits a goddess of the sky rather than the underworld; choose one way and keep to it. Write in your moon record what you promised.",safety:"Never leave a flame unattended, and keep candles away from curtains at an open window. If you are outside after dark, stay somewhere safe and tell someone. Frankincense smoke can affect asthma and pets. Keep lilies away from cats."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Month by month",intro:`Your relationship with Selene now lives in the month itself. Greet her at the Noumenia when the first crescent appears, and at the full moon. Let her rest at the dark of the moon. Keep your moon record going if it helps; many devotees keep one for years.

Once a year, at the full moon nearest your dedication, or at the Mounichia, read your promise again and renew it, change it, or set it down with thanks. If you lose the thread, just look up. She is there most nights, keeping time, and you can begin again at the next crescent.`,days:30,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"selene-ongoing-noumenia",title:"Greet the new crescent",instructions:"At each Noumenia, look for the young moon after sunset, greet her with raised hands and give a little honey-water. Bless the new month.",times:12,every:"month",premade:"folk-noumenia-blessing",prompt:"What do I hope for this month?"},{id:"selene-ongoing-full",title:"Honour the full moon",instructions:"At each full moon, sit in her light for a few minutes, give honey, incense or a white flower, and add a line to your moon record.",times:12,every:"month",prompt:"What has her light shown me this month?"},{id:"selene-ongoing-rededicate",title:"Yearly rededication",instructions:"At the full moon nearest your dedication anniversary, or at the Mounichia, repeat the dedication rite with your promise rewritten for the coming year.",times:1,every:"holy-day",prompt:"What has a year of watching the moon taught me?"}]}],sources:["Homeric Hymn 32, To Selene, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White, Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns and Homerica (1914), Project Gutenberg #348: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/348 (quoted: \u201Cfrom her immortal head a radiance is shown from heaven and embraces earth\u201D; \u201Cat eventime in the mid-month\u201D; \u201Cher great orbit is full and then her beams shine brightest as she increases. So she is a sure token and a sign to mortal men\u201D; \u201Cexceeding lovely amongst the deathless gods\u201D; \u201CHail, white-armed goddess, bright Selene, mild, bright-tressed queen!\u201D)","Hesiod, Theogony 371\u2013374, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), Project Gutenberg #348 (quoted: \u201Cgreat Helius (Sun) and clear Selene (Moon) and Eos (Dawn) who shines upon all\u201D)","Apollonius of Rhodes, Argonautica 4.57\u201358, trans. R. C. Seaton (1912), Project Gutenberg #830: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/830 (quoted: \u201Cnor do I alone burn with love for fair Endymion\u201D)","Orphic Hymn 9 (VIII in Taylor\u2019s numbering), To the Moon, \u201CThe Fumigation from Aromatics\u201D, trans. Thomas Taylor, The Hymns of Orpheus (London, 1792), checked against the archive.org scan: https://archive.org/details/dli.ministry.28841 (quoted, with the long s modernised: \u201CHear, Goddess queen, diffusing silver light\u201D; \u201CFair lamp of Night\u201D)","Pausanias, Description of Greece 3.26.1, 5.1.4\u20135 and 6.24.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones, checked at https://www.theoi.com/Text/Pausanias3B.html and https://www.theoi.com/Text/Pausanias6B.html","Plato, Laws 887e and Gorgias 513a; Aristophanes, Clouds 749\u2013755; Virgil, Georgics 3.391\u2013393; Macrobius, Saturnalia 5.22.9\u201310; Varro, De Lingua Latina 5.68 (Luna Noctiluca); Plutarch, Agis 9 (the oracle of Pasiphae)","Jon D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Civil Calendar of the Athenian Year (1975); H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (1977)","Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (1999); Robert Graves, The White Goddess (1948)"]};return c(u);})();
